#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""tkinterface - build Tk interfaces by drawing them, in the spirit of the
NeXTSTEP Interface Builder.  Python + Tk, standard library only.

Drag objects from the Palette onto the window, arrange them, set their
attributes in the Inspector, then Control-drag from one object to another to
connect them: a control (button, slider, field ...) sends an action to its
target, which can be another object (a label that takes the slider's value,
a progress bar that steps when a timer fires ...) or the Controller, whose
action methods you write in the Inspector.  Control-dragging from the
Controller to an object makes an outlet - a Controller attribute that refers
to that object.

Test Interface runs the window for real, with every connection live and
print() output going to a console.  Generate Code writes a standalone Python
program (an Interface class that builds and wires the window, and your
Controller class); documents are saved as JSON (.tkif).

Usage:  tkinterface [file.tkif]
        tkinterface --build-app [DIR]    build a macOS tkinterface.app (with icon)
        tkinterface --icon-png SIZE [OUT.png]

Mouse (design window):
  click / shift-click / drag on empty space   select
  drag an object or its handles               move / resize
  double-click an object                      edit its text in place
  Control-drag or right-drag                  connect (or use Connect mode)
  drag the window's bottom-right corner       resize the window

Keys (Cmd on macOS, Ctrl elsewhere):
  N O S Shift-S   new / open / save / save as
  R               test the interface        G   generate code
  Z Shift-Z       undo / redo               X C V D A   cut copy paste
                                                        duplicate select-all
  F B             bring to front / send to back
  Delete          delete the selection      arrows  nudge (Shift: by grid)
"""

